<ImageAcquisitionInfo>
    <Help>
        <AdaptorHelp property="BlackReference">The black reference voltage for the A/D converter.

When digitizing from analog video signals, the black and white reference levels
determine the minimum and maximum levels, respectively, of an input voltage
range.  Any voltage below the black reference is interpreted as the minimum
image value and any voltage above the white reference is  interpreted as the
maximum image value.

The property InputGain affects the video signal before the A/D converter
receives it, hence changes the InputGain parameter must be taken into account to
determine the actual 'black' reference point of the incoming video signal.  See
Matrox's MIL-Lite Users Guide, Chapter 7 for more information.<SeeAlso>WhiteReference, InputGain.</SeeAlso>
        </AdaptorHelp>
        <AdaptorHelp property="WhiteReference">The white reference voltage for the A/D converter.

When digitizing from analog video signals, the black and white reference levels
determine the minimum and maximum levels, respectively, of an input voltage
range.  Any voltage below the black reference is interpreted as the minimum
image value and any voltage above the white reference is  interpreted as the
maximum image value.

The property InputGain affects the video signal before the A/D converter
receives it, hence changes the InputGain parameter must be taken into account to
determine the actual 'white' reference point of the incoming video signal.  See
Matrox's MIL-Lite Users Guide, Chapter 7 for more information.<SeeAlso>BlackReference, InputGain.</SeeAlso>
        </AdaptorHelp>
        <AdaptorHelp property="XScaleFactor">Image scale factor for the x-dimension.  

This scale factor affects the width of the acquired image by subsampling the
image in the x-dimension.  The image returned to MATLAB will contain only 1 of
every XSCALEFACTOR columns in the x-dimension.  

This subsampling is applied before the region-of-interest parameter,
ROIPOSITION, is applied, hence the ROIPOSITION dimensions are affected by the
change of this parameter.  

See Matrox's MIL-Lit Users Guide, Chapter 7 for more information.<SeeAlso>YScaleFactor, VideoResolution, ROIPosition.</SeeAlso>
        </AdaptorHelp>
        <AdaptorHelp property="Timer1ActiveTime">The time for the active portion of the exposure timer #1 output.  

This parameter specifies the time, in seconds, in which exposure timer #1
remains in the active state.  This time generally is referred to as
&quot;exposure time&quot; if the output is used to control the exposure of a
camera.  

Upon the triggering of the exposure timer, the output will remain  inactive for
the TIMER1DELAY seconds then become active for  TIMER1ACTIVE seconds before
returning inactive.  The level of the output is determined by the TIMER1MODE
parameter.  The triggering of the exposure timer is dictated by the
TIMER1TRIGGERSOURCE and TIMER1TRIGGERCONDITION parameters.  The exposure timer
will not respond to a trigger unless it is on, see the TIMER1 parameter.   

See the Matrox MIL-Lite Users Guide, chapter 7 under the section &quot;Grabbing
with Triggers and Exposures&quot; for a detailed description of  how to use
exposure timers.  The adaptor presents exposure timers using the manual exposure
bypass model described in the Matrox  documentation.<SeeAlso>Timer1, Timer1Mode, Timer1TriggerSource, Timer1Delay.</SeeAlso>
        </AdaptorHelp>
        <AdaptorHelp property="Timer1Delay">The time delay before exposure time #1's output goes active.  

This parameter specifies the time, in seconds, in which exposure timer #1
remains in the inactive state after a trigger before it enters an active state.  

Upon the triggering of the exposure timer, the output will remain  inactive for
the TIMER1DELAY seconds then become active for  TIMER1ACTIVE seconds before
returning inactive.  The level of the output is determined by the TIMER1MODE
parameter.  The triggering of the exposure timer is dictated by the
TIMER1TRIGGERSOURCE and TIMER1TRIGGERCONDITION parameters.  The exposure timer
will not respond to a trigger unless it is on, see the TIMER1 parameter.   

See the Matrox MIL-Lite Users Guide, chapter 7 under the section  &quot;Grabbing
with Triggers and Exposures&quot; for a detailed description of  how to use
exposure timers.  The adaptor presents exposure timers using the manual exposure
bypass model described in the Matrox documentation.<SeeAlso>Timer1, Timer1Mode, Timer1TriggerSource, Timer1ActiveTime.</SeeAlso>
        </AdaptorHelp>
        <AdaptorHelp property="Timer1TriggerSource">The input trigger source of timer #1.    

This specifies the source to be monitored for a trigger event.  A trigger event
is when the source satisfies the TIMER1TRIGGERCONDITION  condition.  

Upon the triggering of the exposure timer, the output will remain  inactive for
the TIMER1DELAY seconds then become active for  TIMER1ACTIVE seconds before
returning inactive.  The level of the output is determined by the TIMER1MODE
parameter.  The triggering of the exposure timer is dictated by the
TIMER1TRIGGERSOURCE and TIMER1TRIGGERCONDITION parameters.  The exposure timer
will not respond to a trigger unless it is on, see the TIMER1 parameter.   

See the Matrox MIL-Lite Users Guide, chapter 7 under the section  &quot;Grabbing
with Triggers and Exposures&quot; for a detailed description of  how to use
exposure timers.  The adaptor presents exposure timers using the manual exposure
bypass model described in the Matrox  documentation.<SeeAlso>Timer1, Timer1Mode, Timer1TriggerCondition, Timer1ActiveTime, Timer1Delay.</SeeAlso>
        </AdaptorHelp>
        <AdaptorHelp property="Timer1Mode">The output polarity of the active state of exposure timer #1.  

This specifies what output level corresponds to the active state of the exposure
timer.  If set to 'activeHigh', the output will be TTL high during the active
time and TTL low when inactive.    

Upon the triggering of the exposure timer, the output will remain  inactive for
the TIMER1DELAY seconds then become active for  TIMER1ACTIVE seconds before
returning inactive.  The level of the output is determined by the TIMER1MODE
parameter.  The triggering of the exposure timer is dictated by the
TIMER1TRIGGERSOURCE and TIMER1TRIGGERCONDITION parameters.  The exposure timer
will not respond to a trigger unless it is on, see the TIMER1 parameter.   

See the Matrox MIL-Lite Users Guide, chapter 7 under the section  &quot;Grabbing
with Triggers and Exposures&quot; for a detailed description of  how to use
exposure timers.  The adaptor presents exposure timers using the manual exposure
bypass model described in the Matrox documentation.<SeeAlso>Timer1, Timer1TriggerSource, Timer1TriggerCondition, Timer1ActiveTime,
Timer1Delay.</SeeAlso>
        </AdaptorHelp>
        <AdaptorHelp property="Timer1TriggerCondition">The trigger condition detection criteria for exposure timer #1.  

This specifies the condition to be considered a trigger event on the timer's
trigger source, TIMER1TRIGGERSOURCE.  Upon satisfying this condition, the timer
is triggered.  

Upon the triggering of the exposure timer, the output will remain inactive for
the TIMER1DELAY seconds then become active for  TIMER1ACTIVE seconds before
returning inactive.  The level of the output is determined by the TIMER1MODE
parameter.  The triggering of the exposure timer is dictated by the
TIMER1TRIGGERSOURCE and TIMER1TRIGGERCONDITION parameters.  The exposure timer
will not respond to a trigger unless it is on, see the TIMER1 parameter.   

See the Matrox MIL-Lite Users Guide, chapter 7 under the section  &quot;Grabbing
with Triggers and Exposures&quot; for a detailed description of  how to use
exposure timers.  The adaptor presents exposure timers using the manual exposure
bypass model described in the Matrox documentation.<SeeAlso>Timer1, Timer1TriggerSource, Timer1TriggerMode, Timer1ActiverTime, Timer1Delay.</SeeAlso>
        </AdaptorHelp>
        <AdaptorHelp property="Timer1">Enables exposure timer #1 and its output.  

Upon enabling the exposure timer, it will start detection of triggers and
generate output as appropriate.

See the Matrox MIL-Lite Users Guide, chapter 7 under the section  &quot;Grabbing
with Triggers and Exposures&quot; for a detailed description of  how to use
exposure timers.  The adaptor presents exposure timers using the manual exposure
bypass model described in the Matrox  documentation.<SeeAlso>Timer1TriggerSource, Timer1TriggerCondition, Timer1TriggerMode,
Timer1ActiveTime, Timer1Delay.</SeeAlso>
        </AdaptorHelp>
        <AdaptorHelp property="Timer2ActiveTime">The time for the active portion of exposure timer #2 output.  

This parameter specifies the time, in seconds, in which exposure timer #2
remains in the active state.  This time generally is referred to as
&quot;exposure time&quot; if the output is used to control the exposure of a
camera.  

Upon the triggering of the exposure timer, the output will remain  inactive for
the TIMER2DELAY seconds then become active for  TIMER2ACTIVE seconds before
returning inactive.  The level of the output is determined by the TIMER2MODE
parameter.  The triggering of the exposure timer is dictated by the
TIMER2TRIGGERSOURCE and TIMER2TRIGGERCONDITION parameters.  The exposure timer
will not respond to a trigger unless it is on, see the TIMER2 parameter.   

See the Matrox MIL-Lite Users Guide, chapter 7 under the section &quot;Grabbing
with Triggers and Exposures&quot; for a detailed description of  how to use
exposure timers.  The adaptor presents exposure timers using the manual exposure
bypass model described in the Matrox  documentation.<SeeAlso>Timer2, Timer2Mode, Timer2TriggerSource, Timer2Delay.</SeeAlso>
        </AdaptorHelp>
        <AdaptorHelp property="Timer2Delay">The time delay before exposure time #2's output goes active.  

This parameter specifies the time, in seconds, in which exposure timer #2
remains in the inactive state after a trigger before it enters an active state.  

Upon the triggering of the exposure timer, the output will remain  inactive for
the TIMER2DELAY seconds then become active for  TIMER2ACTIVE seconds before
returning inactive.  The level of the output is determined by the TIMER2MODE
parameter.  The triggering of the exposure timer is dictated by the
TIMER2TRIGGERSOURCE and TIMER2TRIGGERCONDITION parameters.  The exposure timer
will not respond to a trigger unless it is on, see the TIMER2 parameter.   

See the Matrox MIL-Lite Users Guide, chapter 7 under the section  &quot;Grabbing
with Triggers and Exposures&quot; for a detailed description of  how to use
exposure timers.  The adaptor presents exposure timers using the manual exposure
bypass model described in the Matrox documentation.<SeeAlso>Timer2, Timer2Mode, Timer2TriggerSource, Timer2ActiveTime.</SeeAlso>
        </AdaptorHelp>
        <AdaptorHelp property="Timer2TriggerSource">The input trigger source of timer #2.    

This specifies the source to be monitored for a trigger event.  A trigger event
is when the source satisfies the TIMER2TRIGGERCONDITION  condition.  

Upon the triggering of the exposure timer, the output will remain  inactive for
the TIMER2DELAY seconds then become active for  TIMER2ACTIVE seconds before
returning inactive.  The level of the output is determined by the TIMER2MODE
parameter.  The triggering of the exposure timer is dictated by the
TIMER2TRIGGERSOURCE and TIMER2TRIGGERCONDITION parameters.  The exposure timer
will not respond to a trigger unless it is on, see the TIMER2 parameter.   

See the Matrox MIL-Lite Users Guide, chapter 7 under the section  &quot;Grabbing
with Triggers and Exposures&quot; for a detailed description of  how to use
exposure timers.  The adaptor presents exposure timers using the manual exposure
bypass model described in the Matrox  documentation.<SeeAlso>Timer2, Timer2Mode, Timer2TriggerCondition, Timer2ActiveTime, Timer2Delay.</SeeAlso>
        </AdaptorHelp>
        <AdaptorHelp property="Timer2Mode">The output polarity of the active state of exposure timer #2.  

This specifies what output level corresponds to the active state of the exposure
timer.  If set to 'activeHigh', the output will be TTL high during the active
time and TTL low when inactive.    

Upon the triggering of the exposure timer, the output will remain  inactive for
the TIMER2DELAY seconds then become active for  TIMER2ACTIVE seconds before
returning inactive.  The level of the output is determined by the TIMER2MODE
parameter.  The triggering of the exposure timer is dictated by the
TIMER2TRIGGERSOURCE and TIMER2TRIGGERCONDITION parameters.  The exposure timer
will not respond to a trigger unless it is on, see the TIMER2 parameter.   

See the Matrox MIL-Lite Users Guide, chapter 7 under the section  &quot;Grabbing
with Triggers and Exposures&quot; for a detailed description of  how to use
exposure timers.  The adaptor presents exposure timers using the manual exposure
bypass model described in the Matrox documentation.<SeeAlso>Timer2, Timer2TriggerSource, Timer2TriggerCondition, Timer2ActiveTime,
Timer2Delay.</SeeAlso>
        </AdaptorHelp>
        <AdaptorHelp property="Timer2TriggerCondition">The trigger condition detection criteria for exposure timer #2.  

This specifies the condition to be considered a trigger event on the timer's
trigger source, TIMER2TRIGGERSOURCE.  

Upon satisfying this condition, the timer is triggered.  Upon the triggering of
the exposure timer, the output will remain inactive for the TIMER2DELAY seconds
then become active for  TIMER2ACTIVE seconds before returning inactive.  The
level of the output is determined by the TIMER2MODE parameter.  The triggering
of the exposure timer is dictated by the TIMER2TRIGGERSOURCE and
TIMER2TRIGGERCONDITION parameters.  The exposure timer will not respond to a
trigger unless it is on, see the TIMER2 parameter.   

See the Matrox MIL-Lite Users Guide, chapter 7 under the section  &quot;Grabbing
with Triggers and Exposures&quot; for a detailed description of  how to use
exposure timers.  The adaptor presents exposure timers using the manual exposure
bypass model described in the Matrox documentation.<SeeAlso>Timer2, Timer2TriggerSource, Timer2TriggerMode, Timer2ActiveMode, Timer2Delay.</SeeAlso>
        </AdaptorHelp>
        <AdaptorHelp property="Timer2">Enables exposure timer #2 and its output.  

Upon enabling the exposure timer, it will start detection of triggers and
generate output as appropriate.

See the Matrox MIL-Lite Users Guide, chapter 7 under the section  &quot;Grabbing
with Triggers and Exposures&quot; for a detailed description of  how to use
exposure timers.  The adaptor presents exposure timers using the manual exposure
bypass model described in the Matrox  documentation.<SeeAlso>Timer2TriggerSource, Timer2TriggerCondition, Timer2TriggerMode,
Timer2ActiveTime, Timer2Delay.</SeeAlso>
        </AdaptorHelp>
        <AdaptorHelp property="DigitalInFormat">Identifies the digital signal format of the digital input.  

This specifies what input format to use for digital inputs.  The options  are
either TTL and a differential input like RS422 or LVDS.  Selecting one format
over another may affect which input on the device needs to be used.  See the
documentation of the device for specifics on which inputs corresponds to which
format.
	</AdaptorHelp>
        <AdaptorHelp property="DigtialOutFormat">Identifies the digital signal format of the digital output.  

This specifies what output format to use for digital outputs.  The options  are
either TTL and a differential input like RS422 or LVDS.  Selecting one format
over another may affect which output on the device needs to be used.  See the
documentation of the device for specifics on which output corresponds to which
format.
        </AdaptorHelp>
        <AdaptorHelp property="UserOutputBit0">Identifies the state of the USER output bit #0.  

When set to 'on', the USER output bit will be a TTL high, otherwise the output
will be TTL low.
	   <DeviceHelp device="Genesis">This output is associated with the USER(0) output pin of the digital interface
connector.</DeviceHelp>
	   <SeeAlso>DigitalOutFormat.</SeeAlso>
        </AdaptorHelp>
        <AdaptorHelp property="UserOutputBit1">Identifies the state of the USER output bit #1.  

When set to 'on', the USER output bit will be a TTL high, otherwise the output
will be TTL low.
	   <DeviceHelp device="Genesis">This output is associated with the USER(1) output pin of the digital interface
connector.</DeviceHelp>
	   <SeeAlso>DigitalOutFormat.</SeeAlso>
        </AdaptorHelp>
        <AdaptorHelp property="UserOutputBit2">Identifies the state of the USER output bit #2.  

When set to 'on', the USER output bit will be a TTL high, otherwise the output
will be TTL low.
	    <DeviceHelp device="Genesis">This output is associated with either the Camera Control Bit 0 (digital input
board, TTL format only) or the Module Load Output (Digital cable adaptor, TTL
format only) output.</DeviceHelp>
	    <SeeAlso>DigitalOutFormat.</SeeAlso>
        </AdaptorHelp>
        <AdaptorHelp property="UserOutputBit3">Identifies the state of the USER output bit #3.  

When set to 'on', the USER output bit will be a TTL high, otherwise the output
will be TTL low.
	    <DeviceHelp device="Genesis">This output is associated with either the Camera Control Bit 1 (digital input
board, TTL format only) or the Module Clock Output (Digital cable adaptor, TTL
format only) output.</DeviceHelp>
	    <DeviceHelp device="Orion">This output is associated with the USER1OUT signal on the video I/O connector.</DeviceHelp>
	    <DeviceHelp device="Corona II">This output is associated with the USER1OUT output signal on the Video Input
Connector. </DeviceHelp>
	    <DeviceHelp device="Meteor II/MC">This output is associated with the USER1OUT output signal on the Video Input
Connector.</DeviceHelp>
	    <DeviceHelp device="Meteor II/Std">This output is associated with the USER1OUT output signal on the Video Input
Connector.</DeviceHelp>
	    <DeviceHelp device="Corona II+Digital">This output is associated with the USER1OUT output signal on the Video Input
Connector.</DeviceHelp>
	    <SeeAlso>DigitalOutFormat.</SeeAlso>
        </AdaptorHelp>
        <AdaptorHelp property="UserOutputBit4">Identifies the state of the USER output bit #4.  

When set to 'on', the USER output bit will be a TTL high, otherwise the output
will be TTL low.
	    <DeviceHelp device="Corona II+Digital">This output is associated with the USER2OUT output signal on the Video Input
Connector.</DeviceHelp>
	    <DeviceHelp device="Genesis">This output is associated with either the Camera Control Bit 2 (digital input
board, TTL format only) or the Module Data Output (Digital cable adaptor, TTL
format only) output.</DeviceHelp>
	    <DeviceHelp device="Orion">This output is associated with the USER2OUT signal on the video I/O connector.</DeviceHelp>
	    <DeviceHelp device="Corona II">This output is associated with the USER2OUT output signal on the Video Input
Connector.</DeviceHelp>
	    <DeviceHelp device="Meteor II/MC">This output is associated with the USER2OUT output signal on the Video Input
Connector.</DeviceHelp>
	    <DeviceHelp device="Meteor II/Std">This output is associated with the USER2OUT output signal on the Video Input
Connector.</DeviceHelp>
	    <SeeAlso>DigitalOutFormat.</SeeAlso>
        </AdaptorHelp>
        <AdaptorHelp property="UserOutputBit5">Identifies the state of the USER output bit #5.  

When set to 'on', the USER output bit will be a TTL high, otherwise the output
will be TTL low.
	    <DeviceHelp device="Corona II+Digital">State of the USER3OUT output signal on the Video Input Connector.</DeviceHelp>
	    <SeeAlso>DigitalOutFormat.</SeeAlso>
        </AdaptorHelp>
        <AdaptorHelp property="UserOutputBit6">Identifies the state of the USER output bit #6.  

When set to 'on', the USER output bit will be a TTL high, otherwise the output
will be TTL low.
	    <DeviceHelp device="Corona II+Digital">State of the USER4OUT output signal on the Video Input Connector.</DeviceHelp>
	    <SeeAlso>DigitalOutFormat.</SeeAlso>
        </AdaptorHelp>
        <AdaptorHelp property="UserOutputBit7">Identifies the state of the USER output bit #7.  

When set to 'on', the USER output bit will be a TTL high, otherwise the output
will be TTL low.

	    <SeeAlso>DigitalOutFormat.</SeeAlso>
        </AdaptorHelp>
        <AdaptorHelp property="UserOutputBit8">Identifies the state of the USER output bit #8.  

When set to 'on', the USER output bit will be a TTL high, otherwise the output
will be TTL low.
	    <SeeAlso>DigitalOutFormat.</SeeAlso>
        </AdaptorHelp>
        <AdaptorHelp property="UserOutputBit9">Identifies the state of the USER output bit #9.  

When set to 'on', the USER output bit will be a TTL high, otherwise the output
will be TTL low.
	    <DeviceHelp device="Corona II">State of the USER3OUT output signal on the Video Input Connector.</DeviceHelp>
	    <SeeAlso>DigitalOutFormat.</SeeAlso>
        </AdaptorHelp>
        <AdaptorHelp property="YScaleFactor">Image scale factor for the y-dimension.  

This scale factor affects the height of the acquired image by subsampling the
image.  The image returned to MATLAB will contain only 1 of every YSCALEFACTOR
rows in the y-dimension.

This subsampling is applied before the region-of-interest parameter,
ROIPOSITION, is applied, hence the ROIPOSITION dimensions are affected by the
change of this parameter.

See Matrox's MIL-Lit Users Guide, Chapter 7 for more information.<SeeAlso>XScaleFactor, VideoResolution, ROIPosition.</SeeAlso>
        </AdaptorHelp>
        <AdaptorHelp property="Hue">The hue level of the video decoder.  

The HUE property allows the adjustment of the color as it is digitized. Changing
this value shifts the image in hue.

For more information, see the documentation that came with the device.<SeeAlso>Saturation, Brightness, Contrast.</SeeAlso>
        </AdaptorHelp>
        <AdaptorHelp property="Saturation">The saturation level of the video decoder.  

The SATURATION property allows the adjustment of the color as it is digitized.
Changing this value shifts the image in saturation.  

For more information, see the documentation that came with the device.<SeeAlso>Hue, Brightness, Contrast.</SeeAlso>
        </AdaptorHelp>
        <AdaptorHelp property="Brightness">The brightness level of the video decoder.  

The BRIGHTNESS property allows the adjustment of the intensity as it is
digitized.  Changing this value shifts the image in intensity.  

For more information, see the documentation that came with the device.<SeeAlso>Contrast, Hue, Saturation.</SeeAlso>
        </AdaptorHelp>
        <AdaptorHelp property="Contrast">The contrast level of the video decoder.  

The CONTRAST property allows the adjustment of the image contrast as it is
digitized.  

For more information, see the documentation that came with the device.<SeeAlso>Brightness, Hue, Saturation.</SeeAlso>
        </AdaptorHelp>
        <AdaptorHelp property="AutomaticInputGain">Enables or disables the automatic adjustment of the input gain.  

When enabled, the frame grabber automatically adjusts the input gain, within
limits of the device, as to produce an average intensity that maybe more
appealing.  The actual algorithm used to determine the input gain is dependent
on the device implementation. 
	 </AdaptorHelp>
        <AdaptorHelp property="VCRInputFilter">Enables improved digitization of video from video cassette recording technologies.  

When enabled, the device improves image stability by treating the sync portion
of the signal as instable, which is typically the case of video from video
cassette players/recorders.
	 </AdaptorHelp>
        <AdaptorHelp property="Timer1ClockSource">The exposure timer #1 pacer clock source.  

This parameter specifies the clock source used for the counting of time for the
TIMER1ACTIVETIME and TIMER1DELAY production.  This  should be set to a source in
which the time can be represented in a multiple of the clock source that can be
represented in the resolution of the exposure timer's counter.  It is
recommended that if you are not sure which is the most appropriate clock source
that 'automatic' should be used, as that setting allows the device driver to
choose the source given the settings of the TIMER1ACTIVETIME and TIMER1DELAY
parameters.  

See the Matrox MIL-Lite Users Guide, chapter 7 under the section  &quot;Grabbing
with Triggers and Exposures&quot; for a detailed description of  how to use
exposure timers.  The adaptor presents exposure timers using the manual exposure
bypass model described in the Matrox documentation.<SeeAlso>Timer1, Timer1Mode, Timer1TriggerSource, Timer1ActiveTime, Timer1Delay.</SeeAlso>
        </AdaptorHelp>
        <AdaptorHelp property="InputFilter">Specifies the lowpass filter to apply to the video input.

When set to something other than 'none', an analog low-pass filter is applied to
the video input before it is sent to the A/D converter. Using a low-pass filter
can improve the acquisition of monochrome images from a color video source.  

See the documentation that was included with your device for particular
information on the various input filters available.
	 </AdaptorHelp>
        <AdaptorHelp property="InputGain">Input video gain.  

The input gain is an analog amplification of the selected video signal before it
reaches the analog-to-digital circuitry.  Changing this value will affect the
video input range digitized.  

The property InputGain effects the video signal before the A/D converter
receives it, hence changes the INPUTGAIN parameter must be taken into account to
while determining the appropriate values of either the
BLACKREFERENCE/WHITEREFERENCE, or the BRIGHTNESS/CONTRAST parameters.  

See Matrox's MIL-Lite Users Guide, Chapter 7 for more information.
	 </AdaptorHelp>
        <AdaptorHelp property="SyncInput">Specifies the source of the sync signals.  

Every source is comprised of two signal types: video and syncl.  The video
portion of the signal contains the imagery information while the sync portion of
signal contains markers of the beginning of frames and lines in in the video
signal.    

While there are two types of signals, they can be combined into a single
physical input.  This type of signal is called a 'composite' signal.  Standard
video inputs like RS170, CCIR, NTSC, or PAL are typically available as a
composite signal, so only one cable contains both types of signal information.
	 </AdaptorHelp>
        <AdaptorHelp property="UserOutputBit10">Identifies the state of the USER output bit #10.

When set to 'on', the USER output bit will be a TTL high, otherwise the output
will be TTL low.
	    <DeviceHelp device="Corona II">State of the USER4OUT output signal on the Video Input Connector.</DeviceHelp>
	    <DeviceHelp device="Corona II+Digital">State of the USER4OUT output signal on the Video Input Connector.</DeviceHelp>
	    <SeeAlso>DigitalOutFormat.</SeeAlso>
        </AdaptorHelp>
        <AdaptorHelp property="Timer2ClockSource">The exposure timer #2 pacer clock source.  

This parameter specifies the clock source used for the counting of time for the
TIMER2ACTIVETIME and TIMER2DELAY production.  This  should be set to a source in
which the time can be represented in a multiple of the clock source that can be
represented in the resolution of the exposure timer's counter.  It is
recommended that if you are not sure which is the most appropriate clock source
that 'automatic' should be used, as that setting allows the device driver to
choose the source given the settings of the TIMER2ACTIVETIME and TIMER2DELAY
parameters.  

See the Matrox MIL-Lite Users Guide, chapter 7 under the section  &quot;Grabbing
with Triggers and Exposures&quot; for a detailed description of  how to use
exposure timers.  The adaptor presents exposure timers using the manual exposure
bypass model described in the Matrox documentation.<SeeAlso>Timer2, Timer2Mode, Timer2TriggerSource, Timer2ActiveTime, Timer2Delay.</SeeAlso>
        </AdaptorHelp>
	<AdaptorHelp property="StartingField">Specifies which field is acquired first.

Most standard video signal formats are transmitted as an interlaced
signal.  Every frame is split into two 'fields' - one field containing the
even lines and one containing odd lines.  The reason for this is 
somewhat historical.  When acquiring interlaced video, the device
will acquire two adjacent incoming fields and put the even field in the
even lines, and the odd field in the odd lines of the acquired image.

Setting this property to 'evenField' causes the even field to be 
considered the first field of the frame.  Setting it to 'oddField'
makes the odd field the first field of the frame.  Setting it to 'any'
causes the first field encountered to be the first field of the frame.
</AdaptorHelp>
<AdaptorHelp property="AutoExposure">Specify the exposure value (EV).
	   	
AutoExposure specifies the exposure value (EV). An EV number defines certain
combinations of aperture and shutter speed settings that determine exposure.
For more information about controlling the AutoExposure feature of a
particular camera, see the documentation that came with the device.
</AdaptorHelp>
<AdaptorHelp property="Focus">Specify the camera-to-subject distance.

Focus is the process of adjusting the distance setting on a camera's lens
system to produce a sharp image. The Focus property specifies the
camera-to-subject distance in meters. For more information about the
focusing features of a specific camera, see the documentation that came with
the device.
</AdaptorHelp>
<AdaptorHelp property="Gain">Specify the gain adjustment.

Gain describes the circuit gain in decibels (dB) with reference to the
original image at 0. For more information about setting the Gain property
for a particular camera, see the documentation that came with the device.
</AdaptorHelp>
<AdaptorHelp property="Gamma">Specify the gamma control.
	   	
The gamma defines the function between the light level input and the light
level picture output. For more information on the light level relation
function and the Gamma value, see the documentation that came with the
device.
</AdaptorHelp>
<AdaptorHelp property="Iris">Specify the camera aperature opening.

A camera's iris (or aperture) is the opening through which light enters the
camera. Camera's provide a mechanism through which you can control the size
of this opening. You specify the size as an F number.  For more information
about controlling the iris of a particular camera, see the documentation
that came with the device. 
</AdaptorHelp>
<AdaptorHelp property="OpticalFilter">Specify the optical filter setting.

OpticalFilter describes the camera's optical lens filter function. For more
information on the optical lens filter and the OpticalFilter value, see the
documentation that came with the device. 
</AdaptorHelp>
<AdaptorHelp property="Pan">Specify camera horizontal positioning.

Pan specifies the horizontal positioning of a camera in degrees.  Positive
values indicate clockwise movement from the origin (the camera rotates
clockwise when viewed from above); negative values indicate counterclockwise
movement from the origin. For more information controlling the panning of a
particular camera, see the documentation that came with the device. 
</AdaptorHelp>
<AdaptorHelp property="Sharpness">Specify the image sharpness.

Sharpness specifies the subjective impression of the amount of detailed
information provided in a picture image. For more information controlling
the sharpness feature of a particular camera, see the documentation that
came with the device.
</AdaptorHelp>
<AdaptorHelp property="Shutter">Specify the amount of time that the sensor is exposed to light.
	   	
The Shutter property specifies the amount of time that the sensor is exposed
to light, also known as integration time. You specify the shutter speed in
seconds. For more information about setting the shutter speed of a
particular camera, see the documentation that came with the device.
</AdaptorHelp>
<AdaptorHelp property="TargetTemperature">Specify target temperature for the device.

TargetTemperature specifies the target temperature at which you want the camera to
operate.  For more information about the temperature capabilities of a
particular camera, see the documentation that came with the device. 
<SeeAlso>TemperatureMode</SeeAlso></AdaptorHelp>
<AdaptorHelp property="Tilt">Specify camera vertical positioning.

Tilt specifies the vertical positioning of the camera in degrees. Positive
values indicate upward movement from the origin; negative values indicate
downward movement from the origin. For more information on controlling tilt
in a particular camera, see the documentation that came with the device. 
</AdaptorHelp>
<AdaptorHelp property="WhiteBalanceU">Specify the U component of the color temperature of white.
   	
The term "white balance" describes how a digital camera adjusts the color
balance to compensate for the ambient lighting. Using the WhiteBalanceU
property, you can specify the color temperature of the U component of the
white value the camera uses to calibrate colors.  For more information about
specifying the White Balance on a particular camera, see the documentation
that came with the device.
<SeeAlso>WhiteBalanceV</SeeAlso>
</AdaptorHelp>
<AdaptorHelp property="WhiteBalanceV">Specify the V component of the color temperature of white.
   	
The term "white balance" describes how a digital camera adjusts the color
balance to compensate for the ambient lighting. Using the WhiteBalanceV
property, you can specify the color temperature of the V component of the
white value the camera uses to calibrate colors.  For more information about
specifying the White Balance on a particular camera, see the documentation
that came with the device.
<SeeAlso>WhiteBalanceU</SeeAlso>
</AdaptorHelp>
<AdaptorHelp property="Zoom">Specify the lens magnification.

Zoom describes the lens magnification. For more information on the
magnification power of a particular camera, see the documentation that came
with the device. 
</AdaptorHelp>
<AdaptorHelp property="SourceCompensation">Set the source compenstation for setting the ROI.

It is possible to optimize grabs by taking advantage of hardware cropping.
This is only availble with cameras the support the DCAM format 7.  Cameras
typically have a limited set of supported ROIs, so for arbitrary ROIs, the ROI
cropping must be done in software.

When set to 'off', this property specifies that the camera should attempt
the ROI cropping.  When set to 'on', the ROI cropping is done in software.
<SeeAlso>ROIPosition</SeeAlso>
</AdaptorHelp>

    </Help>
